  L('m3-3', 'm3', 'Trade-offs between fairness concepts and measures',
`• **Impossibility results** (Kleinberg et al.; Chouldechova): when base rates differ between groups, a classifier generally **cannot satisfy calibration (or predictive parity) and equalised odds at the same time** (except in trivial cases such as a perfect predictor).
• **Fairness vs accuracy**: imposing fairness constraints usually costs some accuracy or profit.
• **Individual vs group fairness** can conflict: equalising group rates may mean treating similar individuals differently.
• **Demographic parity vs merit**: equal approval rates may ignore genuine differences in risk; but "genuine" differences can themselves reflect historical discrimination.
• Different stakeholders favour different measures; legal context matters (disparate treatment vs disparate impact).

Practical stance: there is no single "fair" metric; choose measures **explicitly**, justify them for the use case, document trade-offs and involve legal, ethics and business owners.`,
['Calibration vs equalised odds incompatible when base rates differ', 'Fairness vs accuracy trade-off', 'Individual vs group fairness tension', 'Choose, justify and document metrics']);
