  L('m3-6', 'm3', 'Techniques for making AI more explainable',
`**Global** (whole model) vs **local** (one prediction); **model-specific** vs **model-agnostic**.

• **Intrinsically interpretable models**: linear/logistic regression, scorecards, small trees, rule lists, GAMs.
• **Feature importance**: e.g. permutation importance (drop in performance when a feature is shuffled).
• **Partial dependence plots (PDP)** and **ICE** curves: how predictions change as one feature varies.
• **LIME**: fits a simple **local surrogate** model around one prediction by perturbing inputs.
• **SHAP**: based on **Shapley values** from cooperative game theory; fairly allocates the prediction (vs the average) among features; local contributions add up to the prediction; can be aggregated globally.
• **Counterfactual explanations**: the smallest change to inputs that would change the decision ("if income were €5,000 higher, the loan would be approved") — useful for customers.
• **Global surrogate models**: a simple model trained to mimic the complex one.
• For deep nets: saliency maps, attention visualisation.

Caveats: explanations are approximations, can be unstable or manipulated, and may mislead about causality.`,
