  L('m5-10', 'm5', 'Sources of misinterpretation of model results',
`Even a correct model can lead to wrong decisions if outputs are misunderstood:

• **Correlation vs causation**: feature importance or SHAP values show association, not causal effect.
• **Ignoring uncertainty**: point estimates presented without confidence intervals; overconfident probabilities; poor calibration.
• **Using a model outside its scope** (different population, product or economic regime than training data) — extrapolation.
• **Misreading metrics**: high accuracy on imbalanced data; AUC treated as a probability of correctness; R² as proof of a good model.
• **Misunderstanding explanations**: XAI outputs are approximations; local explanations generalised to the whole model.
• **Anchoring and automation bias**: users accept outputs uncritically; or **confirmation bias**: they only accept outputs that match prior beliefs.
• **Aggregation**: portfolio averages hiding segment problems (Simpson's paradox).
• **Poor communication**: technical outputs not translated for decision-makers; unclear units or horizons (PD one-year vs lifetime).
• **GenAI**: treating fluent text as verified fact.

Remedies: clear documentation of intended use and limitations, user training, uncertainty reporting, plain-language summaries.`,
['Correlation ≠ causation (also for SHAP)', 'Ignoring uncertainty and calibration', 'Use outside scope/extrapolation', 'Misreading metrics and explanations', 'Automation/confirmation bias; Simpson\'s paradox']);
