  L('m5-11', 'm5', 'Governance challenges of GenAI and possible solutions',
`**Challenges**
• **Is it a model?** GenAI may not fit traditional model definitions; many uses are tools embedded in vendor software.
• **Scale and decentralisation**: many use cases across the firm, often small and fast-moving; **shadow AI**.
• **Third-party foundation models**: limited transparency about training data, architecture and updates; vendor changes without notice.
• **Evaluation**: no single ground truth; non-deterministic outputs; hallucinations; benchmark contamination.
• **Data protection and IP**; prompt injection and other security risks.
• **Accountability** for outputs and for **agentic** actions.
• Rapidly changing regulation.

**Solutions**
• Clear **GenAI policy** and acceptable-use rules; inventory of use cases (not only models).
• **Risk-based tiering of use cases** (internal productivity vs customer-facing vs decision-making).
• Use-case–level testing: red-teaming, hallucination and toxicity metrics, human evaluation, guardrails, RAG grounding.
• **Human-in-the-loop** for material outputs; logging and monitoring of prompts/outputs.
• **Vendor due diligence**, contractual rights (no training on your data, change notification), fallback options.
• Cross-functional AI governance committee (risk, compliance, legal, IT security, data, business); staff training; alignment with frameworks such as NIST AI RMF (incl. its GenAI profile) and ISO/IEC 42001.`,
['Model definition, scale, shadow AI', 'Opaque vendor foundation models', 'Hard to evaluate; hallucinations; security', 'Use-case inventory and risk tiering', 'Testing, guardrails, human review, vendor contracts, cross-functional committee']);
